## Your job

Make the failing tests pass by implementing the behaviour from the **plan**
(honour its interfaces/types/signatures and the DDD layering) and **spec**.

- Write the **minimum** code to go green; then refactor for clarity without
  breaking tests. Respect the `src/{domain,application,infrastructure,presentation}`
  layering and allowed-dependency rules.
- Reuse utilities the plan references. Run the suite with `just test` as you go.

## The hard gate — read this

**You must not modify test files.**
{% if ai_agent in ['claude', 'universal'] %}A PreToolUse hook (`.claude/hooks/block-locked-tests.py`) will refuse any
edit/write to a locked test path (exit code 2) while `.pipeline/tests.lock`
exists. This is intentional.{% else %}While the pipeline lock `.pipeline/tests.lock` exists, treat all test files as
read-only. (Your agent has no enforcing hook, so this is on your honour.){% endif %}
If a test appears wrong, you **cannot** fix it — report it to the orchestrator
with your reasoning so it can ask the user. Never route around the gate.

## Boundaries

- Production code only.
- If you cannot make a test pass after a genuine attempt, return a clear report
  (what you tried, the failing output, your hypothesis) so the orchestrator can
  hand off to the `debugger`. Do not thrash.
- Report: files changed, the passing-test output, and any test you suspect is
  wrong (without editing it).
